\section{File format}{

The file is in the
\href{https://huggingface.co/docs/safetensors}{safetensors} format, which
Python (\code{safetensors.numpy.load_file()}), JavaScript and other languages
read too:
\itemize{
\item 8 bytes: the length \code{N} of the header in bytes, an unsigned 64-bit
little-endian integer;
\item \code{N} bytes: the header, one JSON object in UTF-8, padded with spaces so
that \code{N} is a multiple of 8. It has one entry per array, in the order
the arrays are stored, named \code{image} and \code{patches} (for
\code{\link[=embedImages]{embedImages()}}), \code{embeddings} (for a single matrix) or by the names of
the list given. Each entry has \code{dtype} (\code{"F32"} or \code{"F16"}), \code{shape}
(the dimensions, e.g. \verb{[36, 512]}) and \code{data_offsets} (\verb{[begin, end]}:
the array's first byte and the byte after its last, counted from the end
of the header);
\item the arrays' numbers, one array after another with nothing between them,
in row-major (C) order: an \verb{images x d} matrix is stored image by image,
an \verb{images x tiles x d} array image by image and tile by tile.
}

Numbers are IEEE 754 floats of 4 bytes (\code{"F32"}) or 2 bytes (\code{"F16"}), and
always little-endian, whatever the computer that writes or reads the file.

The header's \verb{__metadata__} entry is an object of strings: \code{format}
(\code{"dimoose-embeddings"}), \code{version} (\code{"1"}), \code{single} (\code{"true"} when \code{x}
was one array and not a list, so that it is read back as one),
\verb{rownames.<array>} (a JSON array of the row names: image ids or texts;
other dimensions' names are not kept), \verb{tiles.<array>} and
\verb{empty.<array>} (the tiling and the rule for empty tiles, see
\code{\link[=embedImages]{embedImages()}}, as numbers separated by commas) and \code{model} (a JSON
object with the model's details, if there is a model).

\code{loadEmbeddings()} also reads safetensors files written by other programs,
as long as their arrays are 16-, 32- or 64-bit floats.
}

\section{Precision}{

R keeps numbers as 64-bit floats and the file holds 32-bit ones (about 7
significant digits). Vision models compute in 32 bits, so what
\code{\link[=embedImages]{embedImages()}} and \code{\link[=embedTexts]{embedTexts()}} return is saved and read back exactly;
other numbers come back rounded to the nearest 32-bit float. With
\code{precision = "float16"} every number is rounded to about 3 significant
digits: unit-length embeddings change by at most 0.0005 per coordinate.
Numbers too large for the chosen precision become \code{Inf}, and \code{NA} is read
back as \code{NaN}.
}

\section{Embeddings that come with dimoose}{

The embeddings of the package's example photos are in its
\code{extdata/embeddings} folder, so the examples can be followed with no
Python: \verb{pics-clip-vit-b-32.safetensors} (the 36 fruit photos in
\code{extdata/pics}, whole photos and 4 x 4 tiles),
\verb{new-photos-clip-vit-b-32.safetensors} (the 6 photos in
\code{extdata/new-photos}), \verb{texts-clip-vit-b-32.safetensors} (the prompts of
the vision vignette) and \code{montana-bioclip.safetensors} (the tree photos in
\code{extdata/montana} and the names of \code{\link[=montanaKey]{montanaKey()}}'s species, embedded with
BioCLIP). The folder's \code{README.md} says how each was made.
}

\examples{
# stand-ins for embedImages(model, images): 3 images, 2 tiles each, 4 dimensions
emb <- list(image = matrix(rnorm(12), 3, 4, dimnames = list(c("a", "b", "c"), NULL)),
            patches = array(rnorm(24), c(3, 2, 4), dimnames = list(c("a", "b", "c"), NULL, NULL)))
attr(emb$patches, "tiles") <- 1L
file <- tempfile(fileext = ".safetensors")
saveEmbeddings(emb, file)
back <- loadEmbeddings(file)
all.equal(back$image, emb$image, tolerance = 1e-6)
rownames(back$image)
attr(back$patches, "tiles")

# the example photos' embeddings, made with CLIP ViT-B/32: a key with no Python
pics <- loadEmbeddings(system.file("extdata", "embeddings", "pics-clip-vit-b-32.safetensors",
                                   package = "dimoose"))
dim(pics$patches)
attr(pics, "model")$arch
images <- imageSet(system.file("extdata", "pics", package = "dimoose"))
sniglets <- discoverSniglets(pics$patches, k = 40)
scores <- snigletScores(pics$patches, sniglets$centroids)
keyFromSniglets(scores, images, sniglets, method = "rpart")
}
